<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="portfolio"><title>Portfolio and Reader's Guide</title>
<p>
        During the first week of class, we will discuss as a class what success in this class might look like, and then you will meet individually with me to develop a set of personalized goals. (See <xref ref='important_dates' text='title'/>.)
</p>
<p>
    These goals will not about grades and grading. Instead, you are invited to consider your <em>learning</em>, and more generally what <em>success</em> in this course will be for you.
</p>
<p>
        Over the course of the semester, you will assemble a comprehensive portfolio that demonstrates that you have met the basic requirements for the course and demonstrates your progress toward your goals. Part of this portfolio will be three <em>Progress Checks</em>.
        </p>
<p>In addition to the Progress Checks, you are expected to regularly submit items to me for focused feedback. Based on this feedback, you will reflect, rework, extend, and revise as necessary to meet basic requirements and show forward progress.</p>
        <p>
        At least one time, mid-semester, you will schedule an individual meeting to discuss your progress, your successes, and any challenges you are facing.
</p>
<p>
	For each individual meeting, you will prepare and share with me an agenda or guide ahead of time with specific items you wish to discuss. This guide should discuss successes, challenges, and progress, and should direct the reader where to find evidence of these items. For example, you might include something along the lines of <q><em>Successes</em> Since our last meeting, I have reviewed and improved my understanding of difference quotients and average rate of change. Some good examples of my understanding and interpretation can be found in <em>Driving North/South</em> and <em>Filling a Tank</em>.</q><fn>These titles are completely invented and may not be anything like the titles of activities we actually use.</fn>
</p>
<p>
        Your guide should also address the following questions.
    <ul>
        <li><p>What are you particularly proud of or excited about?</p></li>
        <li><p>What has been challenging?</p></li>
        <li><p>What do you plan to do next?, and</p></li>
        <li><p>What do you need from me (the instructor) to facilitate your progress toward your goals?</p></li>
    </ul>
</p>
<p>
For your final portfolio conference, which will be during the week of final exams, you will submit your final portfolio (with a final version of your reader's guide), again citing items of interest. Then, based on your learning and progress toward your goals and your submitted evidence, you will tell me what grade you have earned for the course.
</p>
</subsection>
